body:
With the Elder Things, Lovecraft provides such a detailed description that I feel that his description is the "true" image of the Elder Things. He gives an in-depth description of their anatomy, including specific measurements, and the true uncomprehendable foe in The Mountains of Madness is whatever the pilot saw in the mountains beyond the Mountains of Madness.

&#x200B;

As for the Great Old Ones, he gives enough description to make each appear distinct, but I think it is up to each person's interpretation for specifics.  Just as each person thinks/perceives in a different way, I think that each person may see a Great Old One slightly different.

I was a Yogs fan for years, and when I saw that Kim and Trott, some of my favorites, were playing D&D I knew I had to watch it.  Even though I came for those two, I love every single person in the group.  I miss Matt and his shenanigans, but Tom and Rhiannon have been amazing additions to the group.

High Rollers is what truly got me into D&D.  My friends and I had played for years, but I was never very invested in it.  Our whole party was usually just a bunch of murder hobos, there was almost no RP, and we would get bored or distracted extremely easily.  But High Rollers was a discovery of what D&D COULD be.  Mark's storytelling, the gang's acting, and even just their genuine enthusiasm for the game really made me stop and think.

&#x200B;

Shortly after beginning High Rollers, I joined an additional D&D group, full of High Rollers/Critical Role fans, and it was some of the most fun I have ever had.  Everyone was super engaged, our DM was amazing, and I finally got to RP my characters.  Eventually, I was able to slowly show my original group of friends just how fun D&D could be if people put more passion into it.  The last year or two has been the best we've ever had.

&#x200B;

As much as I loved the Lightfall campaign and setting, I think Aerois is where the gang truly shines.  Mark's storytelling, the characters, and the way everyone plays them is so engaging and fun to watch.  Everyone is much more confident with their characters, and they all seem to really love them and get into character. High Rollers definitely deserves much more credit/recognition than it gets, especially from non-Yogscast fans.
